            {
              "id": "glog-81fe10cf-d9ef-48d0-a859-6d065c1b55ff",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source data is larger than the destination buffer. This is a common vulnerability in C++ programming that can lead to serious security issues such as data corruption, system crash, or even arbitrary code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `memcpy(p_binary_data, cache.data.data(), b_size)`, the `memcpy` function is copying `b_size` bytes from `cache.data.data()` to `p_binary_data`. If `b_size` is larger than the size of `p_binary_data`, it will result in a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source data. This can be done by checking the size of the source data and the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif (b_size <= sizeof(p_binary_data)) {\n    memcpy(p_binary_data, cache.data.data(), b_size);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: source data is too large for destination buffer\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, `memcpy` is only called if `b_size` is less than or equal to the size of `p_binary_data`. If `b_size` is larger, an error is handled instead.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `memcpy` function.\n- `<vector>` or `<array>`: These libraries provide the `data` function used on `cache.data`.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-9f7aee42-186f-4e9a-8e5a-bff2d4eb4c6a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source buffer is larger than the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, the `memcpy` function is copying 4 bytes from `buf1[12]` to `pmmetrics_version`. If `pmmetrics_version` is not large enough to hold 4 bytes, this can result in a buffer overflow vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`. Alternatively, you can use functions that do check the size of the destination buffer, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif (sizeof(pmmetrics_version) >= 4) {\n    memcpy(&pmmetrics_version, &buf1[12], 4);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, the size of `pmmetrics_version` is checked before calling `memcpy`. If `pmmetrics_version` is not large enough to hold 4 bytes, an error is handled.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so the only library dependency required by the code example is:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-b477b824-e7ce-417d-bbdf-965fd7634e70",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is considered unsafe because it does not guarantee null-termination of the destination string when the source string length is greater than or equal to the destination buffer size. This can lead to buffer overflows, which can be exploited to execute arbitrary code, overwrite data, or cause a system crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination of the destination string. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `std::string` class or the `strlcpy` function if available.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncpy` function with `strlcpy` if available, or use `std::string`:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string message = final_message.str();\nif (message.size() > MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE - 1) {\n    message.resize(MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE - 1);\n}\nmemcpy(&data_item->message, message.c_str(), message.size() + 1);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `memcpy`\n- `<string>` for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-1720da42-f0c4-41e6-9b6a-ab208b57768e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is considered unsafe because it does not guarantee null-termination of the destination string when the source string length is greater than or equal to the destination buffer size. This can lead to buffer overflows, which can be exploited to execute arbitrary code, overwrite data, or cause a system crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination of the destination string. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `std::string` class or the `strlcpy` function if available.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncpy` function with `strlcpy` if available, or use `std::string`:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string message = final_message.str();\nif (message.size() > MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE - 1) {\n    message.resize(MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE - 1);\n}\nmemcpy(&data_item->message, message.c_str(), message.size() + 1);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `memcpy`\n- `<string>` for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
