            },
            {
              "id": "glog-5a869710-4c10-4a63-8dbd-78b251374610",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, if `cpu_set_size * sizeof(uint64_t)` is larger than the size of `cpu_set`, it can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues, including arbitrary code execution, data corruption, and application crashes.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the data and the size of the buffer before calling `memcpy`.\n\nAdditionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy`, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s`, which include a length parameter to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t copy_size = cpu_set_size * sizeof(uint64_t);\nif (copy_size <= sizeof(cpu_set)) {\n    memcpy(cpu_set, bitmask.data(), copy_size);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we calculate the size of the data to be copied and check if it exceeds the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `memcpy` function.\n- `<cstdint>`: This library provides the `uint64_t` type.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-1e3b7a95-222f-4b2a-94a1-91eaa995869f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is considered unsafe because it does not guarantee that the destination string will be null-terminated, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. In the provided code snippet, the `strncpy` function is used to copy a string from `driver_name.c_str()` to `info->driver_name` with a maximum length of `AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1`. If the source string is longer than the destination buffer, the destination string will not be null-terminated, which can lead to undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination of the destination string. In C++, the `std::string` class provides safe string handling functionality. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncpy` function with the `std::string::copy` function, which ensures null-termination of the destination string:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string driver_name_str = driver_name.c_str();\ndriver_name_str.copy(info->driver_name, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1);\ninfo->driver_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::string` class and its methods.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-025fd22d-3af1-4196-ad09-6e6dc766dabf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is considered unsafe because it does not guarantee null-termination of the destination string when the source string length is greater than or equal to the size parameter. This can lead to buffer overflows, which can be exploited to execute arbitrary code, overwrite data, or cause a system crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination of the destination string. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `std::string` class or the `strlcpy` function if available.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncpy` function with `strlcpy` if available, or use `std::string`. Here is an example of how to fix the code using `std::string`:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string version(reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_ver_str));\ninfo->version = version.substr(0, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `cstring` for `strncpy`\n- `string` for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-8290311c-9389-4972-8a1e-d505da68e81e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is considered unsafe because it does not guarantee that a null character will be appended at the end of the destination string. This can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is longer than the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, if the `driver_date` string is longer than `AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1`, the `info->driver_date` string will not be null-terminated, which can lead to undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination of the destination string. In C++, you can use the `std::string` class which automatically handles memory management and ensures null-termination.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncpy` function with `std::string::copy` function. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string driver_date_str = driver_date.substr(0, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1);\ndriver_date_str.copy(info->driver_date, driver_date_str.size());\ninfo->driver_date[driver_date_str.size()] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::string` class and its methods.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "language": "en-US",
